studio.html is the live studio. It is a complete HTML document: doctype, head, body,
the lot. Open it in any browser and it works, with no server and no build step.

Outside Claude, this whole file is what the person opens: Codex, Copilot, Cursor and
Gemini CLI write it to their disk and it opens straight into a browser.

**In Claude, what gets published as an artifact is the same file with the first five lines and the
last two removed.** The artifact host supplies its own doctype, head and body and
refuses a file that brings its own, so publishing strips them:

    the head block here is charset, viewport and a four-line reset, which is exactly
    what the artifact host puts in. Take those off and the two are byte-identical.

It used to be stored the other way round, as the bare fragment. A browser forgives a
fragment and renders it, so that looked fine for months; the previews that sandbox a
file rather than opening it do not, and it came back as a blank white page with the
headings showing and nothing in them. Store the whole document. Strip on the way out.

The CV lives inside it as the CONST CV block near the top of the script. Rebuild that
block from the current markdown whenever the wording changes, then republish. Layouts,
palettes, typesets and the layout CSS are copies of the ones in scripts/render_cv.py
and assets/skins.json, so the preview equals the print. If you change one, change both.

Copy settings prints the render_cv.py command line for whatever was chosen.

The studio also carries, as a const ASKS block, the advertisement atomised into asks
with the line on the current draft that answers each one. Rebuild it the same way the
CV block is rebuilt: read resu/jobs/<id>.md for the requirements, resu/matches/<id>.md
for the evidence, then re-check every claim against the CV markdown as it currently
reads rather than trusting the match file, because the match file does not know what
survived the last edit.
